That is much broader than Pokémon.

For stocks, “shiny hunting” becomes generating many candidate companies, filtering the universe into favorable habitats, recognizing anomalous fundamentals quickly, and intensifying investigation when a genuine outbreak of signals appears.

For science, manipulate experimental conditions so the phenomenon becomes easier to observe instead of endlessly improving post-hoc analysis.

For intelligence/reconnaissance, suppress irrelevant traffic, increase collection density in likely habitats, establish a normal baseline, and flag deviations.

For biology, repeatedly sample ecological niches where the rare phenotype should be enriched rather than sampling uniformly.

For our Luck Surface, it becomes particularly interesting: instead of waiting for improbable opportunities, search for environments where the production rate of interesting anomalies is naturally high—conferences, unusual datasets, estate sales, research repositories, niche communities, transitional markets, newly exposed APIs, abandoned IP, regulatory changes, etc.

And your earlier bookend/delta idea plugs straight into it. The hunter learns a stable “normal” population, repeatedly refreshes the scene, and detects:

S_t \rightarrow \text{refresh} \rightarrow S_{t+1}

Then asks not “What are all these objects?” but:

\boxed{\text{What appeared in } \Delta(S_t,S_{t+1})\text{ that violates expectation?}}

That is essentially shiny hunting as a general intelligence primitive: engineer the search space, accelerate turnover, internalize the baseline, and hunt the delta.

And I think there is an even bigger vein here: recruit expert rare-finders from different substrates—shiny hunters, birders, mushroom hunters, fossil hunters, metal detectorists, astronomers, cybersecurity threat hunters, radiologists, sports scouts, antique pickers—and run Pattern Sans Echoes across them. Whatever search tactics independently survive all those domains are candidates for actual rare-event search invariants.